  <header>
    <div class="header-left">
      <h1>Volumetrics</h1>
      <p class="subtitle">Eats for fullness by choosing lower-calorie-density foods, not by restricting food groups.</p>
    </div>
    <a class="ghost-btn" href="/diets/">← Back to Diets</a>
  </header>

  <p class="field-hint" style="margin:-4px 0 14px;">Since Nawtch already shows you calories per serving when you log foods, you can apply Volumetrics principles directly in your existing <a href="/foods/">food log</a> — just favor the lower-calorie-density option when you have a choice.</p>

  <div class="settings-section">
    <h2 class="settings-section-title">What Is Volumetrics?</h2>
    <div class="settings-section-body">
      <p class="settings-desc">
        Volumetrics is an eating approach organized around a food's energy density — the number of calories
        it packs per gram — rather than around restricting any specific food group. It groups foods into
        rough density tiers and encourages building meals around the lower-density end, favoring water- and
        fiber-rich foods, because these let a person eat a similar volume and feel similarly full while
        taking in fewer calories overall. No foods are off-limits; the approach simply shifts the balance of
        a plate toward foods that are more filling per calorie.
      </p>
    </div>
  </div>

  <div class="settings-section">
    <h2 class="settings-section-title">How It Works</h2>
    <div class="settings-section-body">
      <p class="settings-desc">
        Volumetrics was developed by Dr. Barbara Rolls, the Helen A. Guthrie Chair in Nutrition at Penn State
        University, based on decades of research into satiety and eating behavior. Its central idea is <strong>energy density</strong> — calories per gram of food. People tend to eat a fairly consistent <em>weight</em> of food each day regardless of its calorie content, largely because water and fiber
        add bulk and fullness without adding calories. Choose more foods with a lot of water and fiber per
        calorie, and you can eat a similar volume of food — and feel similarly full — while taking in fewer
        calories overall, without consciously restricting.
        <br /><br />
        Volumetrics groups foods into four rough density tiers rather than banning any of them:
      </p>
      <ul>
        <li><strong>Very low density</strong> — broth-based soups, non-starchy vegetables, most fruits</li>
        <li><strong>Low density</strong> — starchy vegetables, whole grains, legumes, low-fat dairy, lean protein</li>
        <li><strong>Medium density</strong> — meat, cheese, pizza, ice cream, bread, salad dressing</li>
        <li><strong>High density</strong> — chips, crackers, chocolate, cookies, nuts, butter, oil</li>
      </ul>
      <p class="settings-desc">
        The approach isn't to eliminate the higher-density tiers — nuts and oil, for instance, are
        nutritionally valuable in moderation — but to build most meals around the lower-density tiers so
        total calorie intake drops while the amount of food on the plate, and the feeling of fullness, stays
        about the same.
      </p>
    </div>
  </div>

  <div class="settings-section">
    <h2 class="settings-section-title">The Research</h2>
    <div class="settings-section-body">
      <p class="settings-desc">
        Volumetrics is one of the more directly research-backed approaches on this page — it comes out of a
        specific, decades-long university research program rather than being reverse-engineered from a
        popular book.
      </p>
      <strong>Primary trial:</strong> Ello-Martin JA, Roe LS, Ledikwe JH, Beach AM, Rolls BJ. "Dietary energy
      density in the treatment of obesity: a year-long trial comparing 2 weight-loss diets," <em>American
      Journal of Clinical Nutrition</em>, 2007. A year-long RCT in 97 obese women (71 completed): one group
      was coached to reduce fat intake (RF), the other to reduce fat <em>and</em> deliberately increase
      water-rich fruits and vegetables to further lower energy density (RF+FV). The RF+FV group lost
      significantly more weight (7.9±0.9 kg vs. 6.4±0.9 kg) and reported significantly lower hunger — direct
      evidence that lowering energy density helps people stick with a deficit via satiety, not just
      willpower.
      <br /><br />
      <a href="https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/17556681/" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Read the trial →</a>
      <br /><br />
      <strong>The book:</strong> Rolls, B. (with Hermann, M.) (2012). <em>The Ultimate Volumetrics Diet: Smart,
      Simple, Science-Based Strategies for Losing Weight and Keeping It Off.</em> HarperCollins. This is the
      consumer-facing distillation of Dr. Rolls' research program, not a separate primary source — the trial
      above is the underlying evidence.
      <br /><br />
      <strong>Rankings:</strong> U.S. News & World Report has repeatedly placed Volumetrics among its top
      diets for weight loss — Penn State's own reporting on the annual rankings has described it placing in
      the top few weight-loss diets for over a decade running, most recently noted specifically for "fullness
      per calorie." As with any expert-panel ranking, this reflects a panel's structured judgment across
      criteria like ease of use and nutritional adequacy, not a single trial outcome.
    </div>
  </div>

  <div class="settings-section">
    <h2 class="settings-section-title">Food Examples by Density</h2>
    <div class="settings-section-body">
      <p class="settings-desc">
        <strong>Very low density (build meals around these):</strong> broth-based vegetable soup, leafy
        greens, cucumber, tomatoes, berries, melon, non-fat plain yogurt.
        <br /><br />
        <strong>Low density (good everyday staples):</strong> potatoes, brown rice, oats, beans and lentils,
        skinless chicken breast, fish, low-fat cheese.
        <br /><br />
        <strong>Medium density (fine in reasonable portions):</strong> lean beef, regular cheese, whole eggs,
        bread, pasta, salad dressing.
        <br /><br />
        <strong>High density (enjoy in smaller amounts, not eliminated):</strong> nuts, seeds, dried fruit,
        oils and butter, chips, chocolate, cookies.
      </p>
    </div>
  </div>

  <div class="settings-section">
    <h2 class="settings-section-title">A Sample Day</h2>
    <div class="settings-section-body">
      <p class="settings-desc">
        <strong>Breakfast:</strong> oatmeal made with water or non-fat milk, topped with berries.
        <br /><br />
        <strong>Lunch:</strong> a large vegetable-and-bean salad with a light vinaigrette, plus a piece of
        fruit.
        <br /><br />
        <strong>Dinner:</strong> a broth-based vegetable soup as a starter, followed by grilled chicken or
        fish with a large portion of roasted vegetables and a smaller portion of rice.
        <br /><br />
        <strong>Snack:</strong> a small handful of nuts or a square of dark chocolate — the higher-density
        tier, in a controlled portion rather than eliminated.
      </p>
    </div>
  </div>

  <div class="settings-section">
    <h2 class="settings-section-title">Who It Might Suit</h2>
    <div class="settings-section-body">
      <p class="settings-desc">
        Tends to suit people who find restriction-based dieting hard to sustain because they end up hungry —
        the appeal here is eating a similar volume of food, and feeling similarly full, for fewer calories.
        It requires some attention to a food's water and fiber content rather than following a fixed food
        list, which suits people who like understanding the "why" behind a recommendation more than people
        who want simple binary rules. It may not suit anyone looking for a fully hands-off plan, since some
        awareness of energy density (or, practically, calories per serving) is part of how it works day to
        day.
      </p>
    </div>
  </div>

